{
  "dom_id": "step-q315235",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "In a lab experiment, the temperature of a chemical reactor (in ${}^{\u2218}C$) after $t$ minutes is given by the function $T(t).$ The values of $T(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| **$t$** | $1.2$ | $1.6$ | $2.0$ | $2.4$ | $2.8$ | $3.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$T(t)$** | $28$ | $30$ | $32$ | $34$ | $35$ | $36$ |\n\nApproximate the reactor\u2019s temperature instantaneous rate of change at $t=2.0$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=0.4.$\n\nThe reactor\u2019s temperature at $t=2.0$ is changing at approximately \n{{field-1}} ${}^{\u2218}C$ per minute.",
  "worked_solution": "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{T}^{\u2032}(t)\u2248\\frac{T(t+h)-T(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat time $t=2.0$ with step size $h=0.4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{\u2032}(2.0) & \u2248\\frac{T(2.0+0.4)-T(2.0-0.4)}{2(0.4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{T(2.4)-T(1.6)}{0.8} \\\\  & =\\frac{34-30}{0.8} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{0.8} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the reactor\u2019s temperature at $t=2.0$ is changing at approximately $5\\,{}^{\u2218}C$ per minute.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
